comments.text:
@WesRoth I love your content and don't mean this offensively in any way. more and more, on channels such as yours, I am seeing the major limitations that are inherent with overly simplified one shot prompts during testing, that in fact are just bad prompting given today's standards. often bad results during tests are a results of crappy prompts, but then blamed on the defects or incapability of the model. again and again, by minimally fleshing out the simplified prompts with a bit more information and adhering to common prompt engineering practices that give the model what it needs to provide a solid and best result, I am able to easily improve results.

previously maybe this was ok, but these days it just doesn't cut it. just as the models have evolved, so has prompting. I think you do the models, and your testing,  a bit of a dis-service by giving such a wide lane of a naive and simplified prompt that is open to too much interpretation and variability and doesn't provide enough of the information the model needs.  "create a simulation of a 3d city traffic grid" is an example, but there are much better ones. maybe the defense of this is that the "interpretation and variability" choices are exactly what we want to see, but I think this can still be achieved with better prompting and we will improve the kind of  choices and decisions we see. 

please consider upgrading the quality of your prompts, as you would in working with the models in real life, so your testing results are more meaningful. this is a very large gamut of improvements we are talking about so even a small step in the right direction is better. no need to slum it in the prompt shanties.  :)
